[38;5;231m⏺[39m [1mFifth fork: what a notification [3mis[0;1m on screen — and whether it persists.[0m

  This decides whether notifications are stored objects or transient UI. Storing them means a records table with
  read-state; not storing them means a notification missed is a notification gone forever.

  - [1mBell + inbox, with toasts for live arrivals[0m — a header bell with an unread count, a panel listing notifications with
    read/unread state, and a brief toast when one arrives while you're looking. Buys history, "what did I miss", and a
    per-notification read flag. Costs: stored notification records, read-state transitions, and a decision about how
    long to keep them.
  - [1mInbox only, no toasts[0m — same store, no interruption. Buys calm and less UI. Costs: changes happening in front of you
    go unremarked until you open the panel, which reads as sluggish.
  - [1mToasts only[0m — purely ephemeral, nothing stored. Buys the smallest implementation by a wide margin, no read-state, no
    retention policy. Costs: close the tab and your notifications are gone; there's no "tasks I care about changed
    while I was away", which is most of the value you asked for.

  [1mMy recommendation: bell + inbox with toasts.[0m Your phrasing — "get notified when tasks they care about change" —
  implies not missing things, and only a stored inbox delivers that. One related default I'll write into the design
  unless you object: [1mcoalescing[0m, so five rapid edits to the same task become one notification that says "3 changes"
  rather than five rows. That affects the stored shape, so I'd rather decide it now than bolt it on.
